\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\begin{document}
Triangle $ABC$ has $AB = BC = 5$ and $AC = 6$. Let $E$ be the foot of the altitude from $B$ to $\overline{AC}$ and let $D$ be the foot of the altitude from $A$ to $\overline{BC}$. Compute the area of triangle $DEC$.

Let $h$ be the altitude from $B$ to $AC$. Since $AB = BC$, triangle $ABC$ is isosceles. Thus, $E$ is the midpoint of $AC$, so $AE = EC = \frac{6}{2} = 3$. By the Pythagorean Theorem in right triangle $ABE$, $BE = \sqrt{AB^2 - AE^2} = \sqrt{5^2 - 3^2} = \sqrt{25 - 9} = \sqrt{16} = 4$.
The area of triangle $ABC$ is $\frac{1}{2} \cdot AC \cdot BE = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 6 \cdot 4 = 12$.
Also, the area of triangle $ABC$ is $\frac{1}{2} \cdot BC \cdot AD$, so $12 = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 5 \cdot AD$, which gives $AD = \frac{24}{5}$.
Since $AD$ is the altitude from $A$ to $BC$, triangle $ADC$ is a right triangle with $AC=6$ and $AD = \frac{24}{5}$. Then $CD = \sqrt{AC^2 - AD^2} = \sqrt{6^2 - (\frac{24}{5})^2} = \sqrt{36 - \frac{576}{25}} = \sqrt{\frac{900-576}{25}} = \sqrt{\frac{324}{25}} = \frac{18}{5}$.
Since $E$ is the midpoint of $AC$, $AE = EC = 3$. Then $DE = |CE - CD| = |3 - \frac{18}{5}| = |\frac{15-18}{5}| = \left|\frac{-3}{5}\right| = \frac{3}{5}$.
The area of triangle $DEC$ is $\frac{1}{2} \cdot EC \cdot DE = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 3 \cdot \frac{3}{5} = \frac{9}{10}$.
The area of triangle $DEC$ is $\frac{9}{10} \text{ square units }$.

\end{document}
\boxed{9/10}

