\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\begin{document}

In how many ways can 8 people sit around a round table if 3 of the people -- Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas -- all want to sit together? (Two seatings are considered the same if one is a rotation of the other.)

Let us consider Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas as a single unit. Then we have 6 entities to arrange around the round table: this group of 3 and the other 5 people.
The number of ways to arrange $n$ distinct objects in a circle is $(n-1)!$.
So, the number of ways to arrange these 6 entities around the round table is $(6-1)! = 5! = 120$.
However, within the group of 3 people (Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas), they can be arranged in $3! = 6$ ways.
Therefore, the total number of ways to arrange the 8 people around the round table such that Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas sit together is $5! \times 3! = 120 \times 6 = 720$.

\end{document}
