\begin{enumerate}
    \item When counting from $3$ to $201$, $53$ is the $51^\mathrm{st}$ number counted.
    \item When counting backwards from $201$ to $3$, $53$ is the $n^\mathrm{th}$ number counted.

    We want to find $n$.

    In the first counting sequence, we have the numbers $3, 4, 5, \dots, 201$.
    The number of terms is $201 - 3 + 1 = 199$.
    The $51^\mathrm{st}$ term is $3 + (51 - 1) = 3 + 50 = 53$.

    In the second counting sequence, we have the numbers $201, 200, 199, \dots, 3$.
    The number of terms is $201 - 3 + 1 = 199$.
    The $n^\mathrm{th}$ term is $201 - (n - 1) = 201 - n + 1 = 202 - n$.
    We want $202 - n = 53$.
    $n = 202 - 53 = 149$.

    Therefore, $n = 149$.
\end{enumerate}
\boxed{149}
