Here is the step-by-step solution:

We are asked to divide $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $x^2-3x+2$.

First, we set up the synthetic division. The divisor is $x^2-3x+2$.
The coefficients of the divisor are 1, -3, 2.

\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & & +2 \\
\cline{2-6}
x^2-3x+2 & x^4 & -3x^3 & +4x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^4} & -3x^3 & +2x^2 \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 0 & 2x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2x^2 & -6x & +4 \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & 17x & -13 \\
\end{array}
So, $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = (x^2-3x+2)(x^2+2) + (17x-13)$.

The quotient is $x^2+2$ and the remainder is $17x-13$.

\boxed{17x-13}
