Let $f(x)$ be a function defined on $\mathbb{R}$.
We say that $f$ is an even function if $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.
We say that $f$ is an odd function if $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

If $f$ is both even and odd, then $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f(-x) = -f(x)$.
Thus, $f(x) = -f(x)$, which implies $2f(x) = 0$, so $f(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.
Therefore, there is only one function that is both even and odd, which is the zero function.

The number of functions that are both even and odd is 1.
\boxed{1}

