We are asked to find the least possible remainder when $a^2+b^2$ is divided by 3.
Since $a$ and $b$ are not multiples of 3, we can write $a \equiv 1 \text{ or } 2 \pmod 3$ and $b \equiv 1 \text{ or } 2 \pmod 3$. 
If $a \equiv 1 \pmod 3$, then $a^2 \equiv 1^2 \equiv 1 \pmod 3$. 
If $a \equiv 2 \pmod 3$, then $a^2 \equiv 2^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod 3$. 
So $a^2 \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ for any integer $a$ not divisible by 3. The same is true for $b$. 
Therefore, $a^2 + b^2 \equiv 1 + 1 \equiv 2 \pmod 3$. 

The remainder when $a^2+b^2$ is divided by 3 is 2.

